::: {.doc-subject}

Add a lollipop graph to the current graph.
:::

::: {.doc-usage-source}
Usage

[Source](https://github.com/rich-iannone/edgecase/blob/main/python/edgecase/graph.py#L9588-L9718){target="_blank" rel="noopener"}
:::

::: {.doc-signature .doc-function}
```pythonsignature
Graph.add_lollipop_graph(
    m,
    n,
    type=None,
    label=True,
    rel=None,
)
```
:::

::: {.doc-text}

![Lollipop graph](../assets/graphs/add-lollipop-graph.light.svg){.lightbox fig-alt="Three lollipop graphs side by side: a triangle with a one-node tail for m=3 and n=1, a complete graph on four nodes with a two-node tail, and a complete graph on five nodes with a two-node tail. The tail is a path hanging off one node of the clique." width="100%"}

Creates a complete graph ("candy") of `m` nodes with a path ("stick") of `n` more nodes
hanging off one of them. The graph has `m + n` nodes and `m(m - 1)/2 + n` edges. With
`n=0` it is just the complete graph.

The clique is created first and the stick follows, attached to the clique's last node,
so the far end of the stick is the last node created. In a directed graph every edge
points from the earlier-created node to the later one, so the stick points away from the
clique. The graph is appended without touching or connecting to existing nodes, and the
whole construction is recorded as a single step.

Lollipop graphs are classic extreme cases:

- random walks: with `m` about `2(m + n)/3`, a walk started in the clique takes the
  longest possible expected time (of order `(m + n)^3`) to reach the end of the stick
- mixing a dense core and a long periphery in one test graph, e.g. for centrality,
  k-core, or eccentricity measures
- checking that path-based measures handle a long tail correctly
:::

## Parameters {.doc-parameters}

::: {.doc-definition-items}
<code>[m]{.doc-parameter-name}[:]{.doc-parameter-annotation-sep} [int]{.doc-parameter-annotation}</code>

:   Number of nodes in the clique. Must be at least `2`.

<code>[n]{.doc-parameter-name}[:]{.doc-parameter-annotation-sep} [int]{.doc-parameter-annotation}</code>

:   Number of nodes on the stick. Must be non-negative.

<code>[type]{.doc-parameter-name}[:]{.doc-parameter-annotation-sep} [Optional[str]]{.doc-parameter-annotation} [=]{.doc-parameter-default-sep .op} [None]{.doc-parameter-default}</code>

:   Optional value stored in the `type` attribute of every new node. If `None` (default),
    no `type` attribute is set.

<code>[label]{.doc-parameter-name}[:]{.doc-parameter-annotation-sep} [bool]{.doc-parameter-annotation} [=]{.doc-parameter-default-sep .op} [[True]{.va}]{.doc-parameter-default}</code>

:   If `True` (default), each new node is labelled with its node ID as a string; a label
    that is already used raises `ValueError` before anything is added. If `False`, the
    nodes are left unlabelled.

<code>[rel]{.doc-parameter-name}[:]{.doc-parameter-annotation-sep} [Optional[str]]{.doc-parameter-annotation} [=]{.doc-parameter-default-sep .op} [None]{.doc-parameter-default}</code>

:   Optional value stored in the `rel` attribute of every new edge. If `None` (default),
    no `rel` attribute is set.
:::

## Returns {.doc-returns}

::: {.doc-definition-items}
<code> [Graph]{.doc-parameter-annotation}</code>

:   Self for method chaining. The graph is modified in place.
:::

## Raises {.doc-raises}

::: {.doc-definition-items}
<code> [ValueError]{.doc-parameter-annotation}</code>

:   If `m=` is less than `2` or `n=` is negative.
:::

## See Also {.doc-see-also}

[](`~add_tadpole_graph`) 

:   Add a cycle with a path hanging from it.

[](`~add_barbell_graph`) 

:   Add two cliques joined by a path.

[](`~add_full_graph`) 

:   Add a complete graph.

## Examples {.doc-examples}

A lollipop graph is a complete graph of `m` nodes with a path of `n` nodes, the stick,
hanging from it. A clique of four with a stick of three has `7` nodes and `6 + 3 = 9`
edges:

::: {#67bcb770 .cell execution_count=1}
``` {.python .cell-code}
import edgecase as ec

graph = ec.Graph().add_lollipop_graph(m=4, n=3)

graph.count_nodes(), graph.count_edges()
```

::: {.cell-output .cell-output-display execution_count=1}
```
(7, 9)
```
:::
:::


The stick hangs from node `"4"`, the clique's last node, and ends at node `"7"`, so the
longest shortest path runs from the clique to that tip:

::: {#5813115d .cell execution_count=2}
``` {.python .cell-code}
graph.diameter(), graph.get_degree("4"), graph.get_degree("7")
```

::: {.cell-output .cell-output-display execution_count=2}
```
(4, 4, 1)
```
:::
:::


### A short stick and a long one

Lollipops are the classic worst case for random walks: a walk that starts in the clique
keeps circling inside it and takes a long time to reach the tip. A clique of five with a
stick of one on the left, and with a stick of three on the right, which moves the tip from
two steps from the far side of the clique to four:

::: {#978f821b .cell execution_count=3}
``` {.python .cell-code}
short = ec.Graph().add_lollipop_graph(m=5, n=1)
long = ec.Graph().add_lollipop_graph(m=5, n=3)

short.diameter(), long.diameter()
```

::: {.cell-output .cell-output-display execution_count=3}
```
(2, 4)
```
:::
:::


